<DocsLayout title="Capability matrix" description="The evidence boundary for each dataset modality in super-charge-ml." current="capabilities">
  <p class="eyebrow"><span></span> Support boundary</p>
  <h1>Know what is<br /><em>actually supported.</em></h1>
  <p class="lead">A parser is not a production capability. A dataset family is supported only when ingestion, curation, modelling, evaluation, packaging, inference, monitoring, documentation, and recovery have evidence.</p>

  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Read the label literally.</strong> <code>supported</code> means the complete lifecycle is proven for the declared scope. <code>experimental</code> means you can explore it with explicit opt-in. <code>extension-only</code> means the integration boundary exists, but this beta makes no lifecycle guarantee.</p></div>

  <h2>Current beta matrix</h2>
  <p class="table-hint">On a narrow screen, scroll the matrix horizontally to read the full evidence boundary.</p>
  <div class="capability-table" role="region" aria-label="Capability support matrix" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Dataset family</th><th>Current label</th><th>What exists</th><th>What is missing</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Tabular library path</th><td><span class="status status-supported">Supported*</span></td><td>Readers, profiling, curation gates, leakage-aware splits, task-aware evaluation, bundles, inference, and monitoring contracts.</td><td>Independent clean-clone and full fault-plan evidence before production-proven status.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Text and documents</th><td><span class="status status-experimental">Experimental</span></td><td>Document boundaries, encoding checks, OCR-aware foundations, and provenance structures.</td><td>End-to-end modelling, evaluation, packaging, serving, monitoring, and recovery evidence.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Images</th><td><span class="status status-experimental">Experimental</span></td><td>Bounded decoding, safety limits, metadata, content hashes, deduplication, and baseline features.</td><td>Model lifecycle, saliency validation, serving, drift, and resource recovery evidence.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Audio and video</th><td><span class="status status-extension">Extension-only</span></td><td>Validated encoded samples, explicit codec/probe boundaries, temporal metadata, hashes, and bounded profiles.</td><td>Temporal splitting, models, resource budgets, explanations, serving, drift, and recovery.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Graph and geospatial</th><td><span class="status status-experimental">Experimental</span></td><td>Graph structures, GeoJSON geometry validation, CRS-aware profiles, raster identity, and bounded data foundations.</td><td>Complete lifecycle contracts and independent evidence for production use.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Warehouse and event streams</th><td><span class="status status-extension">Extension-only</span></td><td>Injected DB-API and bounded object connectors establish transport seams.</td><td>Watermarks, late data, deduplication, exactly-once recovery, and warehouse-specific evidence.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="meta">* The supported claim is intentionally narrow: the explicitly gated tabular library workflow, not every possible tabular source or model.</p>

  <h2>The lifecycle gate</h2>
  <figure class="diagram">
    <figcaption class="diagram-caption"><span>SUPPORT DECISION</span><strong>One missing stage keeps the claim below supported.</strong></figcaption>
    <div class="flow-row">
      <div class="flow-node"><span>01</span><b>Declare</b><small>modality · task · scope</small></div>
      <i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node"><span>02</span><b>Evidence</b><small>tests · artifacts · failures</small></div>
      <i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node active"><span>03</span><b>Review</b><small>independent decision</small></div>
      <i class="flow-arrow">→</i>
      <div class="flow-node"><span>04</span><b>Publish</b><small>label · limits · owner</small></div>
    </div>
  </figure>
  <p>Declarations are checked before training. They name required extras, resources, known failure modes, limitations, and evidence links. An incomplete declaration cannot be promoted by calling it experimental; the missing stages remain visible.</p>

  <h2>What to add when expanding support</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Start from raw fixtures: valid, empty, corrupt, oversized, shifted, duplicated, leaked, and privacy-sensitive inputs.</li>
    <li>Implement the modality-neutral contracts first: snapshot identity, curation findings, split identity, lineage, release evaluation, and explanation metadata.</li>
    <li>Run the same 15-stage lifecycle: ingestion, validation, profiling, curation, splitting, feature preparation, modelling, evaluation, data explainability, model explainability, packaging, inference, monitoring, documentation, and recovery/resource tests.</li>
    <li>Only then change the capability label, and attach the command, artifact, digest, reviewer, and limitations to the evidence index.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="callout"><p><strong>Contributor rule:</strong> if a new model works only because the data contract is bypassed, it is not an expansion of support. It is an unreviewed exception.</p></div>
</DocsLayout>

